+(duration-type)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a
+{class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole number of seconds, like
+`120`, or one or more integers each followed by a unit:
+
+- `d` for days,
+- `h` for hours,
+- `m` for minutes,
+- `s` for seconds.
+
+Each unit may be used at most once, and units must be given in that order, so
+`1h30m` and `1d2h3m4s` are valid but `30m1h` and `1h1h` are not. Units are
+case-insensitive. Spaces, negative numbers, and decimals like `1.5h` are
+rejected. A {class}`~datetime.timedelta` passed as a default is used as-is.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
